1
CHAPTER 1
INTRODUCTION
Reading is one of the most fundamental academic skills a child can develop, serving as the 
foundation for learning across all subject areas, including Mathematics, Science, Language, and 
Social Studies. Foundational literacy developed during the early years of education enables learners 
to participate successfully in future learning and supports lifelong educational achievement. 
UNESCO (2025) emphasizes that foundational literacy is a core building block of quality education 
and should be developed during the early years of schooling to promote academic success and 
lifelong learning.
Reading intervention refers to targeted instructional strategies designed to support learners 
who experience difficulties in developing foundational reading skills. Through early identification, 
continuous assessment, and appropriate instructional support, reading intervention helps learners 
strengthen their reading proficiency. A systematic review by Goldfeld et al. (2022) found that 
structured early reading interventions improve oral language and early reading outcomes among 
preschool and primary-grade learners. In the Philippines, strengthening reading intervention has 
become increasingly important due to continuing literacy challenges. According to the Philippine 
Statistics Authority (PSA, 2025), the results of the 2024 Functional Literacy, Education, and Mass 
Media Survey (FLEMMS) showed that although most Filipinos possess basic literacy skills, 
functional literacy remains considerably lower, highlighting the need to strengthen literacy 
development through effective reading interventions. Likewise, the Second Congressional 
Commission on Education (EDCOM II, 2024) identified weak foundational literacy as one of the 
country's most pressing education challenges. It emphasized the importance of improving earlygrade reading interventions.
Despite these efforts, several challenges continue to affect the implementation of reading 
intervention in Philippine public elementary schools. Learners demonstrate varying levels of 
reading proficiency, fluency, vocabulary, and comprehension, making it difficult for teachers to 
address individual learning needs. Teachers are expected to assess learners' reading proficiency, 
monitor their progress, implement appropriate interventions, and manage regular classroom 
instruction. The Philippine Informal Reading Inventory (Phil-IRI) requires teachers to conduct 
individual oral reading assessments to determine learners' reading levels and recommend 
appropriate interventions (Department of Education, 2024). However, teacher workload, large class 
sizes, and numerous ancillary responsibilities often limit the timely delivery of reading 
interventions (EDCOM II, 2024). In addition, maintaining regular communication with parents 

2
regarding learners' reading progress remains a challenge, reducing opportunities to reinforce 
reading practice at home (Hernandez & Decena, 2024).
These challenges highlight the need for more effective and technology-supported reading 
interventions. Recent developments in educational technology have created opportunities to 
improve reading intervention through adaptive learning, learner monitoring, and increased learner 
engagement. Alrawashdeh et al. (2024) found that adaptive learning technologies have a significant 
positive effect on reading literacy by providing instruction that responds to learners' individual 
learning needs. Similarly, Alqahtani (2024) reported that technology-based reading interventions 
improve reading outcomes among elementary learners with reading difficulties. Furthermore, 
Wang, Harun, and Yuan (2024) concluded that gamified reading instruction enhances learner 
motivation, engagement, and reading comprehension when appropriately implemented. Despite 
these advancements, existing reading support systems remain fragmented, often providing 
assessment, digital reading activities, or learner monitoring as separate functions. This 
fragmentation limits teachers' ability to efficiently implement reading interventions and monitor 
learner progress through a unified platform, highlighting the need for an integrated AI-powered 
reading intervention system specifically designed for Philippine public elementary schools.
To address this need, this study proposes TaraBasa AI, an AI-powered adaptive reading 
intervention system with gamified learning for Grade 1 to Grade 3 learners in Philippine public 
elementary schools. The proposed system integrates AI-assisted oral reading assessment, adaptive 
reading intervention, learner progress monitoring, gamified learning activities, and parent 
communication within a single web and mobile platform. By combining these features, TaraBasa 
AI supports teachers in delivering timely, data-driven, and learner-centered reading interventions 
while enabling parents to monitor and reinforce their children's reading development at home. 
Through the development and evaluation of the proposed system, this study aims to contribute a 
practical and technology-driven solution that strengthens the delivery of adaptive reading 
interventions and supports the improvement of English reading proficiency among Grade 1 to 
Grade 3 learners.
Rationale of the study 
Reading literacy is a fundamental component of educational development because it 
enables learners to understand written information, acquire knowledge, and participate effectively 
in academic activities. During the early years of education, learners develop foundational reading 
skills—including word recognition, fluency, vocabulary, and comprehension—that influence their 
future learning. Castles et al. (2018) explained that reading development is a complex process 

3
requiring learners to successfully acquire multiple skills before achieving reading proficiency. 
However, many learners worldwide continue to experience difficulties in developing these essential 
skills. The World Bank, UNESCO, UNICEF, FCDO, USAID, and the Bill & Melinda Gates 
Foundation (2022) reported that learning poverty remains a significant global concern, with many 
children in low- and middle-income countries unable to read and understand simple, ageappropriate texts by age 10. This crisis is most severe in Sub-Saharan Africa, where the learning 
poverty rate increased from 86 percent in 2019 to an estimated 89 percent following pandemicrelated school disruptions. Likewise, World Bank et al. (2022) reported that learning poverty in 
South Asia increased from 60 percent before the pandemic to a projected 78 percent, while Latin 
America and the Caribbean experienced one of the largest increases, from approximately 50 percent 
to about 80 percent. These figures indicate that the global learning crisis is concentrated most 
severely in specific regions and countries.
Across global education systems, identifying and addressing reading difficulties remains a 
persistent challenge. Learners with difficulties in reading fluency, vocabulary, and comprehension 
often struggle to understand academic content and achieve expected learning outcomes. Duke and 
Cartwright (2021) emphasized that effective reading instruction requires evidence-based 
approaches that address the multiple components of literacy, while Peng et al. (2024) highlighted 
that timely interventions are necessary to prevent persistent reading difficulties. Furthermore, 
UNESCO (2025) emphasized that strengthening foundational literacy is essential because reading 
proficiency serves as the basis for lifelong learning, educational achievement, and social 
participation. These conditions highlight the continuing need for effective reading intervention 
during the early grades.
In the Philippines, reading literacy remains a major educational concern as many learners 
continue to experience difficulties in achieving expected literacy competencies. The Philippine 
Statistics Authority (2024), through the Functional Literacy, Education and Mass Media Survey 
(FLEMMS), reported continuing concerns regarding functional literacy among Filipinos, 
indicating difficulties in understanding and applying written information in everyday situations. 
Similarly, the Second Congressional Commission on Education (2024) identified foundational 
learning gaps, including literacy, as among the major challenges affecting the Philippine education 
system. These findings demonstrate the continuing need to strengthen literacy development among 
Filipino learners.
Within Philippine public elementary schools, teachers continue to face challenges in 
identifying learners with reading difficulties and providing appropriate interventions based on their 
reading abilities. The Department of Education (2024), through the Philippine Informal Reading 

4
Inventory (Phil-IRI), requires teachers to assess learners' reading performance, determine reading 
levels, and recommend appropriate interventions. However, Abocejo (2022) identified challenges 
related to reading assessment implementation and addressing the diverse reading needs of learners. 
Likewise, Ersando et al. (2025) found that reading comprehension difficulties remain evident 
among Filipino early elementary learners, emphasizing the need for targeted instructional support 
to improve literacy outcomes.
Similar concerns are evident in Cebu, where learners continue to demonstrate differences 
in reading proficiency and comprehension performance. Po et al. (2025) found variations in the 
reading comprehension abilities of Grade 3 learners in Cebu City, highlighting the need for 
intervention strategies that address learners' specific reading needs. These findings indicate that 
local schools continue to face challenges in identifying learners requiring additional reading 
support and providing timely interventions based on assessment results.
Despite existing literacy programs and assessment practices, gaps remain in addressing the 
diverse reading needs of early-grade learners. Reading assessment and intervention continue to rely 
heavily on teacher observation and manual monitoring, making it difficult to provide individualized 
support, continuously monitor learner progress, and adjust intervention strategies as learners' 
reading abilities change. Alrawashdeh et al. (2024) emphasized that adaptive learning responds to 
individual learner differences by providing learning experiences tailored to learners' specific needs. 
These challenges demonstrate the need for more responsive, technology-supported reading 
intervention and continuous learner monitoring.
The relevance of this study is rooted in the continuing challenges teachers face in assessing 
reading performance, providing individualized interventions, monitoring learner progress, and 
strengthening parent involvement during the early grades. While existing reading intervention 
programs and classroom assessment practices contribute to improving reading skills, there remains 
a need for a comprehensive platform that integrates AI-assisted oral reading assessment, adaptive 
reading intervention based on learners' Phil-IRI reading levels, gamified learning, teacher 
monitoring, and parent communication within a single system designed specifically for Philippine 
public elementary schools. TaraBasa AI: An AI-Powered Adaptive Reading Intervention System 
with Gamified Learning for Grade 1–3 Learners responds to these challenges through an integrated 
platform that combines speech recognition-based reading assessment, adaptive intervention 
recommendations, gamified learning activities, teacher monitoring tools, and automated parent 
notifications. By consolidating these features into a single platform, the proposed system supports 
the timely identification of learners requiring intervention, provides personalized reading activities 
based on learner performance, enhances learner engagement through gamification, and strengthens 

5
collaboration between teachers and parents. In doing so, TaraBasa AI aims to improve the English 
reading proficiency of Grade 1 to Grade 3 learners in Philippine public elementary schools while 
contributing to the advancement of technology-assisted literacy intervention.
Objectives of the study 
This study aims to develop TaraBasa AI: An AI-Powered Adaptive Reading Intervention 
System with Gamified Learning for Grade 1–3 Learners. The system uses AI-powered speech 
recognition to assess learners' English reading proficiency and provide adaptive reading 
interventions through gamified learning activities. 
Specifically, this study aims to:
1. To gather data on the English reading difficulties, instructional needs, and reading 
intervention practices of Grade 1–3 learners through surveys and interviews with 
teachers and parents. 
2. To determine an AI-powered speech recognition mechanism capable of assessing 
learners' English reading proficiency. 
3. To integrate an AI-assisted adaptive reading intervention mechanism that delivers 
gamified reading activities and learning recommendations based on learners' assessed 
reading proficiency. 
4. To implement a real-time monitoring and progress-reporting module that enables 
teachers and parents to track learners' reading performance, intervention progress, and 
reading achievements.
Scope and Limitations of the Study
This study focuses on the development and evaluation of TaraBasa AI, an AI-powered 
adaptive reading intervention system with gamified learning designed for Grade 1 to Grade 3 
learners in Philippine public elementary schools. The study covers English reading assessment and 
intervention, and involves teachers, learners, and parents as primary participants in assessing 
reading proficiency, delivering reading activities, and monitoring reading progress. The current 
focus of the study is a selected public elementary school in Cebu City, where the system will be 
implemented and evaluated as an initial phase. This initial implementation serves as the starting 
point for testing the system's feasibility and effectiveness, with the intention that TaraBasa AI may 
eventually be implemented nationwide in other Philippine public elementary schools in future work 
beyond the current study.
The TaraBasa AI platform integrates AI-assisted speech recognition for oral reading 
assessment, adaptive learning to personalize reading activities according to learners' reading 

6
performance, gamified learning to enhance learner engagement and motivation, and analytics to 
assist teachers in monitoring learners' reading development. The system consists of a mobile 
application for teachers and parents, as well as a web-based dashboard for teachers and system 
administrators. The mobile application enables teachers to conduct AI-assisted oral reading 
assessments, assign adaptive gamified reading activities, monitor learners' reading progress, and 
communicate reading updates to parents. Parents can access their child's reading progress, receive 
automated notifications, and monitor assigned reading activities. The web-based dashboard allows 
teachers and administrators to manage learner records, user accounts, reading materials, assessment 
results, system-generated reports, and other administrative functions. Progress reports and learner 
analytics are integrated into the system to support data-driven instructional decision-making.
The study is limited to the development and evaluation of TaraBasa AI within its current 
implementation in Cebu City and does not examine its long-term effects on learners' English 
reading proficiency, academic achievement, reading motivation, or overall literacy development. 
Likewise, the study does not evaluate large-scale or nationwide institutional adoption, integration 
with existing Department of Education information systems and reading programs, or external 
factors such as digital literacy, device availability, internet connectivity, and home learning 
environments. While these factors may influence the effectiveness and future scalability of the 
system, they are beyond the scope of the present study.
The application requires a stable internet connection to access its full functionality on both 
mobile and web platforms. The AI-assisted speech recognition component is designed to support 
English oral reading assessment and may produce inaccurate results due to variations in learners' 
pronunciation, speech clarity, background noise, microphone quality, internet connectivity, or 
limitations of the speech recognition model. Consequently, assessment results generated by the 
system are intended to assist teachers and should not replace professional judgment in evaluating 
learners' reading performance. Furthermore, the adaptive reading activities are generated based on 
learners' recorded reading performance and are limited to the instructional content available within 
the system.
Despite these limitations, the study aims to develop a functional AI-powered adaptive 
reading intervention system that supports teachers in conducting efficient reading assessment, 
delivering personalized reading intervention, monitoring learners' reading progress, and 
strengthening collaboration between schools and parents. By integrating AI-assisted oral reading 
assessment, adaptive learning, gamified reading activities, learner analytics, and parent 
notifications into a unified web and mobile platform, TaraBasa AI seeks to provide an accessible, 
practical, and curriculum-aligned solution — with Cebu City serving as the current implementation 

7
site and a foundation for nationwide implementation in Philippine public elementary schools in the 
future.
Significance of the Study 
The development and implementation of TaraBasa AI is expected to provide meaningful 
benefits to various stakeholders in the Philippine educational system.
Learners. The primary beneficiaries of TaraBasa AI are Grade 1 to Grade 3 learners. The 
system provides AI-assisted English reading assessment and adaptive gamified learning activities 
that match each learner's reading ability. Through game-based features such as points, badges, 
rewards, and levels, learners are encouraged to practice reading regularly while improving their 
reading skills through engaging and personalized learning experiences.
Teachers. TaraBasa AI assists teachers by providing AI-generated reading assessments, 
real-time progress monitoring, and comprehensive learner reports. These features help teachers 
identify struggling readers, monitor reading development, and make informed instructional 
decisions while reducing the time required for manual reading assessments.
Parents. Parents and guardians benefit from regular notifications regarding their child's 
reading progress, allowing them to stay informed and become more actively involved in supporting 
reading development at home. The system strengthens communication between schools and 
families by providing timely updates on learners' reading performance.
Future Researchers. This study may serve as a valuable reference for future researchers 
conducting related studies on artificial intelligence, reading intervention systems, educational 
technology, and early literacy development. It may also provide a foundation for further 
improvements and expanded implementation of AI-assisted reading intervention systems.
