Figure 1:Flow of the Study
Figure 1 illustrates the conceptual flow of the TaraBasa AI study. The process begins with 
gathering data on the English reading difficulties and intervention needs of Grade 1 to Grade 3 

9
learners as observed by teachers and parents. Data are collected through surveys, interviews, and a 
review of related literature to identify the functional and technical requirements necessary for 
developing the proposed system.
The next phase focuses on determining the algorithms that will be integrated into the 
system. An AI speech recognition algorithm is identified to assess learners' English oral reading 
proficiency by evaluating pronunciation, reading accuracy, and fluency. An adaptive learning 
algorithm is likewise determined to personalize reading activities according to each learner's 
reading proficiency and performance, ensuring that learners receive appropriate reading 
interventions based on their assessment results.
The study follows the Agile Software Development Methodology, which consists of 
planning, requirements analysis, system design, development, testing, deployment, and 
maintenance. This iterative approach enables continuous system improvement through regular
testing, evaluation, and user feedback throughout the development process.
The output of the study is TaraBasa AI: An AI-Powered Adaptive Reading Intervention 
System with Gamified Learning for Grade 1 to Grade 3 Learners. The proposed system is accessible 
through both mobile and web platforms. It integrates AI-assisted English reading assessment, 
adaptive reading intervention, gamified reading activities, learner progress monitoring, and teacher 
and parent access. The system is designed to assist teachers in identifying struggling readers, 
implementing appropriate reading interventions, monitoring learners' reading progress, and 
strengthening collaboration with parents to support the continuous development of learners' English 
reading proficiency.
Definition of Terms 
This section defines the following terms as used in this study.
Adaptive Learning System. It refers to the feature of TaraBasa AI that automatically 
provides reading activities suited to a learner's assessed English reading proficiency based on the 
results of the AI-assisted oral reading assessment.
Artificial Intelligence (AI) Speech Recognition. It refers to the technology integrated into 
TaraBasa AI that analyzes learners' oral reading, converts spoken English into text, and assists 
teachers in evaluating reading accuracy, pronunciation, and fluency.
English Reading Proficiency. It refers to the learner's ability to read English passages 
accurately, fluently, and with understanding as determined through the AI-assisted oral reading 
assessment in TaraBasa AI.

10
Gamified Learning. It refers to the integration of game elements such as points, badges, 
levels, rewards, and achievement indicators within TaraBasa AI to encourage learner engagement 
and sustained participation in reading activities.
Learners. It refers to the elementary pupils who use TaraBasa AI as the primary 
beneficiaries of the adaptive reading intervention system.
Parent Notification System. It refers to the feature of TaraBasa AI that automatically 
informs parents or guardians about their child's reading assessment results, assigned reading 
activities, and reading progress.
Reading Fluency. It refers to one of the reading performance indicators measured by 
TaraBasa AI, focusing on the learner's ability to read English text accurately, smoothly, and at an 
appropriate pace during oral reading assessment.
Speech Recognition Engine. It refers to the AI component of TaraBasa AI responsible for 
processing learners' oral reading, converting speech into text, and generating assessment results to 
support teachers in evaluating reading performance.
System Administrator. It refers to the authorized user responsible for managing user 
accounts, learner records, reading materials, system reports, and other administrative functions 
within TaraBasa AI.
Teachers. It refers to Grade 1 to Grade 3 teachers who use TaraBasa AI to conduct oral 
reading assessments, assign adaptive reading activities, monitor learners' reading progress, and 
review system-generated reports.

11
CHAPTER II
REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE AND STUDIES
This chapter presents a review of existing literature and studies relevant to the development 
of TaraBasa AI: An AI-Powered Adaptive Reading Intervention System with Gamified Learning 
for Grade 1–3 Learners. The review includes the theoretical foundations, related technologies, and 
previous research that support and influence the current study. It aims to establish the context of 
the study, identify existing research gaps, and provide the rationale for the design and development 
of the proposed system. The discussion is organized into the theoretical background, related 
literature, related studies, and a comparative matrix summarizing the key features, limitations, and 
relevance of previous works to the current study.
Theoretical Background
This study is anchored on the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), developed by Fred 
Davis (1989), which explains how users come to accept and use technology. The model proposes 
that two primary beliefs, perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, influence an individual's 
intention to adopt a technological system. Perceived usefulness refers to the extent to which users 
believe that a system enhances their performance, while perceived ease of use refers to the degree 
to which users believe that the system is easy to learn and operate. In the context of TaraBasa AI, 
TAM provides the theoretical basis for understanding how teachers and parents perceive the 
proposed system as a tool for supporting English reading intervention. The system is designed to 
simplify reading assessment, learner monitoring, and communication between schools and parents 
while providing adaptive reading support for learners. Davis (1989) explains that technologies 
perceived as useful and easy to use are more likely to be accepted and integrated into users' daily 
activities. Thus, TAM supports the development of TaraBasa AI as a practical and user-friendly 
educational technology for Philippine public elementary schools.
Supporting the Technology Acceptance Model is the Zone of Proximal Development 
(ZPD) developed by Lev Vygotsky (1978), which explains that learning occurs most effectively 
when learners are given tasks that are slightly beyond their current level of ability but can still be 
accomplished with appropriate guidance or support. The theory emphasizes that instruction should 
be aligned with learners' developmental readiness to maximize learning and promote continuous 
improvement. In relation to TaraBasa AI, ZPD provides the educational foundation for the system's 
adaptive reading intervention. Through an AI-assisted English reading assessment, the system 
identifies each learner's reading proficiency and recommends reading activities appropriate to the 
learner's current ability. As learners demonstrate improvement, the system gradually increases the 

12
difficulty of the reading activities, while learners who require additional support continue to receive 
appropriate intervention. By adapting reading instruction according to individual learner needs, 
TaraBasa AI enables learners to progress within their Zone of Proximal Development and develop 
stronger English reading skills.
Another theory that supports the development of TaraBasa AI is the Mastery Learning 
Theory, developed by Benjamin Bloom (1968), which explains that learners achieve better 
educational outcomes when they are allowed sufficient time, appropriate instruction, and 
continuous feedback before advancing to more complex learning objectives. Rather than requiring 
all learners to progress at the same pace, the theory emphasizes that learners should first 
demonstrate mastery of prerequisite skills before moving to higher levels of learning. In the context 
of TaraBasa AI, this theory supports the system's adaptive learning approach by ensuring that 
learners receive reading activities based on their demonstrated reading proficiency. Learners who 
successfully master a reading level are provided with progressively more challenging activities, 
while those who require additional practice continue receiving appropriate intervention until 
mastery is achieved. This approach promotes individualized learning and helps strengthen 
foundational English reading skills before learners proceed to more advanced reading tasks.
These theories provide the theoretical foundation for TaraBasa AI and guide the 
development of its adaptive reading intervention system. The Technology Acceptance Model 
(Davis, 1989) supports the development of a system that is useful, accessible, and acceptable to 
teachers and parents. The Zone of Proximal Development (Vygotsky, 1978) provides the 
educational basis for personalizing reading activities according to learners' current reading abilities, 
while the Mastery Learning Theory (Bloom, 1968) reinforces continuous assessment and learner 
progression based on demonstrated mastery. By integrating these theories into its framework, 
TaraBasa AI aims to become a user-centered and learner-centered adaptive reading intervention 
system that supports the improvement of English reading proficiency among Grade 1 to Grade 3 
learners in Philippine public elementary schools.
Related Literature 
Reading is widely recognized as one of the most essential skills children develop in their 
early years of schooling because it forms the basis of learning across all academic areas. Through 
reading, learners gain knowledge, strengthen language skills, improve critical thinking, and express 
ideas more effectively. Duke & Cartwright (2021) argued that reading is a complex process 
requiring both word recognition and language comprehension, enabling learners not only to decode 
text but also to construct meaning from it. Castles et al. (2018) added that reading development is 

13
gradual, with children progressively building the skills needed to become fluent, independent 
readers.
Reading proficiency is built upon mastery of foundational literacy skills developed in the 
early years of schooling, including phonological awareness, phonics, vocabulary, decoding, 
fluency, and comprehension. Ehri (2020) showed that children first learn the connection between 
letters and sounds before they can read words accurately and automatically, while Petscher et al. 
(2020) found that successful reading depends on the coordinated growth of several literacy 
components rather than one isolated skill. The Department of Education’s MATATAG Curriculum 
reflects these same foundational competencies, placing strong emphasis on their gradual 
development in the early grades (Department of Education, 2024a). This skill set is increasingly 
treated as a distinct construct in its own right, one that learners must establish before they can 
engage independently with more complex, subject-area texts, a distinction reinforced 
internationally through the Global Coalition for Foundational Learning, launched under the United 
Nations Transforming Education Summit and endorsed by major education and development 
organizations worldwide (UNESCO, n.d.). This is precisely why foundational literacy is treated as 
the starting point for the present review.
Struggling readers need structured, evidence-based support to strengthen foundational 
literacy skills before gaps widen, rather than the same uniform instruction given to every learner 
regardless of need. Alqahtani (2024) found that technology-based reading interventions produced 
a moderate overall effect on the reading skills of elementary students with reading difficulties, 
supporting their use as targeted, evidence-based instruction. Peng et al. (2024) further found that 
combining specific comprehension strategies produced stronger outcomes for struggling readers 
than instruction built around a single strategy, underscoring the importance of selecting effective 
intervention content over relying on volume alone.
Adjusting the difficulty, sequence, and pace of learning content to a learner’s demonstrated 
performance, rather than delivering the same material to every learner regardless of ability, has 
repeatedly been linked to stronger outcomes than static instruction. Gligorea et al. (2023), 
reviewing artificial intelligence and machine learning integration in e-learning, found that this kind 
of adaptive algorithm personalizes learning experiences by optimizing learning pathways, 
improving engagement, retention, and academic performance in the process. Wang, Huang, et al. 
(2024) explained that AI-based adaptive learning improves academic achievement by giving 
learners personalized pathways and immediate feedback based on mastery, while Alrawashdeh et 
al. (2024) found that personalized and adaptive technologies increase learner engagement by 
keeping activities aligned with individual needs. Continuous performance monitoring, dynamic 

14
content adjustment, and individualized pacing distinguish this approach from static digital learning 
materials.
Applying game-design elements, such as points, badges, levels, rewards, and progress 
indicators, to otherwise repetitive instructional tasks has been shown to increase learner motivation 
and participation. Rivera & Garden (2021), synthesizing gamification and student engagement 
literature into a conceptual framework, characterized this application of game elements as a way to 
influence students’ behavioral, cognitive, and affective engagement. Sailer & Homner (2020) 
explained that gamified environments improve motivation, engagement, and academic 
performance by making learning more enjoyable and rewarding. Wang, Harun, & Yuan (2024) 
found that gamification strategies encourage active participation and measurable learning gains 
across reading instruction settings, while Li & Chu (2021) demonstrated that game-based reading 
activities significantly improve children’s reading motivation and willingness to practice. Together, 
these constructs describe both the skills learners must acquire and the strategies available to support 
that acquisition, but their value becomes clearer only against the persistent reading challenges 
examined next.
Across global education systems, reading literacy remains a persistent concern. The World 
Bank, UNESCO, UNICEF, FCDO, USAID, & Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation (2022) reported 
that learning poverty remains a significant global concern, with many children in low- and middleincome countries unable to read and understand simple age-appropriate texts by age ten. Evans & 
Hares (2021) similarly observed that despite years of schooling, many children and youth 
worldwide fail to master basic literacy and numeracy skills, a pattern they linked to gaps in the 
quality, rather than merely the availability, of early instruction. UNESCO (2025) added that 
strengthening foundational literacy remains essential because reading proficiency serves as the 
basis for lifelong learning, educational achievement, and social participation. The problem, then, is 
not confined to any single country but continues across education systems worldwide.
At the learner level, reading difficulties typically stem from weaknesses in one or more 
foundational components, decoding, vocabulary, fluency, or comprehension, rather than a single 
isolated cause. Duke et al. (2021) noted that weaknesses in either word recognition or language 
comprehension limit a learner’s ability to construct meaning from text, often resulting in poor 
overall reading performance. Petscher et al. (2020) added that successful reading depends on the 
coordinated development of several literacy components rather than one skill developing in 
isolation, while Ehri (2020) found that learners who have not yet mastered systematic letter-sound 
relationships struggle to decode unfamiliar words accurately. This compounds difficulty as 

15
instruction shifts from learning to read toward reading to learn, making early identification and 
multidimensional support essential.
In the Philippines, reading literacy remains a major educational concern. The Philippine 
Statistics Authority (2025), through the 2024 Functional Literacy, Education, and Mass Media 
Survey, reported that although most Filipinos possess basic literacy skills, functional literacy, the 
ability to apply reading skills to everyday situations, remains considerably lower, revealing a gap 
between basic and applied literacy competence. The Second Congressional Commission on 
Education (2024) identified weak foundational literacy as among the most pressing challenges 
facing the Philippine education system, citing inconsistent early-grade reading outcomes across 
regions. The Department of Education (2025) acknowledged similar concerns even while reporting 
gains under the MATATAG Curriculum, noting that functional literacy remains a continuing area 
requiring strengthened early intervention. Curricular reform alone, then, has not yet resolved the 
country’s reading literacy challenges.
Within Philippine public elementary schools, the Philippine Informal Reading Inventory 
(Phil-IRI) requires teachers to conduct individual oral and silent reading assessments to determine 
learners’ reading levels and recommend corresponding remediation activities (Department of 
Education, 2024b). Its administration, scoring, and progress monitoring, however, remain largely 
manual, making continuous assessment difficult for teachers managing large classes with many 
struggling readers. This difficulty is compounded by broader workload pressures. Jomuad et al. 
(2021), in a study of elementary teachers, found that heavy workloads are significantly associated 
with higher levels of burnout and reduced capacity to sustain consistent instructional quality, a 
finding directly relevant to teachers tasked with both regular instruction and individualized reading 
assessment. Peng et al. (2024) likewise noted that combining multiple intervention strategies 
effectively requires considerable teacher expertise, planning time, and instructional resources, 
resources that are frequently limited in real classroom settings. Current reading assessment and 
intervention practices, as a result, place a substantial and often unsustainable burden on teachers.
Beyond instructional and assessment challenges, learner motivation plays a critical role in 
reading development. Children who are motivated to read are more likely to participate in literacy 
activities, practice independently, and persist through difficulty. Ryan & Deci (2020) argued that 
learners show stronger engagement when they experience competence, autonomy, and meaningful 
participation during learning, conditions often difficult to sustain in repetitive, remediation-focused 
reading drills. Guthrie & Wigfield (2000) found that learners who view reading as meaningful and 
enjoyable typically demonstrate better engagement and stronger reading performance over time. 
This bidirectional relationship was confirmed more recently by Rettig & Schiefele (2023), whose 

16
longitudinal eye-tracking study of Grade 1 to Grade 3 learners found that reading motivation 
measured early in the school year predicted gains in reading efficiency and comprehension months 
later. Repeated failure in reading tasks, conversely, may reduce learner confidence and lead to 
avoidance behavior, further compounding existing difficulties. These problems, together, call for a 
coordinated response pairing effective pedagogy with practical, scalable teacher support, beginning 
with the educational solutions already in use and the technologies that extend them.
Interdisciplinary research has repeatedly established that skilled reading develops through 
the coordinated growth of decoding and language comprehension, rather than through exposure to 
text alone. Castles et al. (2018) synthesized evidence across many studies to show that reading 
acquisition follows a structured progression from novice to expert reading, a progression Duke et 
al. (2021) extended by outlining specific comprehension-instruction practices consistent with this 
developmental sequence. Ehri (2020) demonstrated that systematic phonics instruction helps 
learners understand letter-sound relationships, directly improving word recognition and decoding 
accuracy, while Duke & Cartwright (2021) argued that comprehension develops through the 
interaction of language comprehension and word recognition. This evidence base, known 
collectively as the Science of Reading, underlies most structured, sequential literacy programs used 
today.
In the Philippine context, the Department of Education has operationalized Science of 
Reading principles through the MATATAG Curriculum, which prioritizes phonological awareness, 
phonics, word recognition, fluency, vocabulary, and comprehension in the early grades 
(Department of Education, 2024a). The curriculum represents a structured, government-mandated 
approach to foundational literacy instruction intended to standardize early-grade reading outcomes 
nationwide, and it sets the framework within which any classroom reading intervention, 
technology-supported or otherwise, must remain curriculum-aligned.
Beyond curriculum standards, the Department of Education relies on the Philippine 
Informal Reading Inventory as its standard assessment tool for identifying learners’ reading levels 
and planning corresponding intervention activities (Department of Education, 2024b). Through oral 
and silent reading assessments, teachers classify learners according to reading ability and assign 
appropriate remediation. Phil-IRI provides a nationally recognized structure for identifying 
struggling readers; its heavily manual administration, however, limits its practical scalability across 
large classrooms, a constraint already noted in the discussion of teacher workload above.
Progressing learners to new content only after they demonstrate sufficient competence in 
prerequisite skills, rather than advancing them on a fixed schedule, reflects what Bloom (1968) 
proposed decades ago: that most learners can achieve high levels of achievement when given 

17
sufficient time and appropriately individualized support. Applied to reading instruction, this 
principle, known as mastery learning, supports the idea that learners should advance through 
foundational skills, such as phonics or vocabulary, only once genuine competence has been 
demonstrated, an approach well suited to structuring adaptive reading intervention.
Adjusting instructional content, process, and pacing to accommodate the diverse learning 
needs, readiness levels, and interests of individual learners within the same classroom traces back 
to Vygotsky’s (1978) concept of the zone of proximal development, which proposed that learners 
benefit most from instruction and scaffolding pitched slightly beyond their current independent 
ability. Puzio, Colby, & Algeo-Nichols (2020), in a meta-analysis of literacy intervention studies, 
found that this kind of differentiation in instructional content, process, and product produced 
positive effects on literacy outcomes such as comprehension, fluency, decoding, and vocabulary, 
supporting the model’s use as classrooms grow increasingly diverse. Alqahtani (2024) reinforced 
this by showing that reading interventions responsive to individual learner needs consistently 
outperform generic, one-size-fits-all approaches, though consistent classroom implementation 
remains difficult without structured support.
As an extension of motivational research, gamified reading activities apply points, badges, 
rewards, and progress indicators directly to literacy practice rather than relying solely on repetitive 
drills. Li & Chu (2021) demonstrated that game-based reading activities significantly improve 
children’s reading motivation and willingness to practice independently. Wang, Harun, & Yuan 
(2024), in a systematic review of gamified reading instruction, found consistent evidence of 
enhanced motivation, engagement, and comprehension when gamification was appropriately 
implemented, and Sailer & Homner (2020) confirmed that gamified environments improve 
academic performance by making learning more enjoyable. Of the educational solutions reviewed 
in this theme, gamified reading activities most directly answer the motivation and engagement 
challenges raised earlier.
Although the Science of Reading, the MATATAG Curriculum, Phil-IRI, mastery learning, 
differentiated instruction, and gamified reading activities each contribute an evidence-based 
component of effective literacy instruction, their consistent implementation remains difficult in 
practice. Roberts et al. (2021) observed that struggling readers, including English learners, often 
require intensive, individualized intervention delivered in small-group or one-to-one settings, a 
level of support that whole-class instruction and manual assessment tools cannot reliably sustain 
across large Philippine classrooms. Pedagogically sound as these approaches are, their dependence 
on teacher capacity, time, and manual monitoring points toward a need for technology capable of 
automating assessment and sustaining engagement at scale, the focus of the next section.

18
Artificial intelligence has increasingly been applied across education to automate 
instructional functions once dependent entirely on teacher effort. Wang et al. (2024), in a systematic 
literature review spanning over 2,000 studies, found that AIED applications cluster around four 
main categories: adaptive learning and personalized tutoring, intelligent assessment and 
management, learner profiling and prediction, and emerging generative-AI tools, with adaptive 
learning and assessment consistently producing the strongest reported gains. Mustafa et al. (2024) 
added that integrated AI systems improve learner achievement, engagement, and instructional 
efficiency more effectively than isolated digital tools. Both reviews caution, however, that many 
existing educational technologies still focus on only one or two instructional functions rather than 
offering complete learning support, a limitation directly relevant to reading intervention platforms.
Building on the general application of AI in education, AI-powered adaptive learning 
systems personalize the content, sequence, and pace of instruction according to real-time learner 
performance data. Wang et al. (2024) found that AI-enabled adaptive learning systems improve 
learner outcomes while giving teachers timely data for instructional decision-making. Alrawashdeh 
et al. (2024) similarly found that personalized and adaptive learning technologies increase learner 
engagement in reading by keeping activities aligned with individual needs, and Gligorea et al. 
(2023) confirmed that AI/ML-driven adaptive systems consistently optimize learning pathways 
across e-learning contexts more broadly. Adaptive learning, on this evidence, is a technically 
mature approach ready for application to Philippine reading intervention.
Evaluating pronunciation, reading accuracy, and fluency directly from a learner’s spoken 
voice is possible through automatic speech recognition, a second relevant technology for this 
review. Kheddar et al. (2024) reported that advances in deep learning have substantially improved 
the accuracy and efficiency of automatic speech recognition, increasingly suiting it for educational 
applications. Pack et al. (2024) found that AI-based automated scoring can achieve validity and 
reliability approaching that of human raters, indicating that AI-driven assessment tools can 
meaningfully reduce the assessment workload placed on teachers. Applied to oral reading 
assessment, this technology speaks directly to the manual, time-intensive nature of Phil-IRI 
administration noted earlier.
Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting learner performance data to support instructional 
decision-making, an approach known as learning analytics, offers a third relevant technology for 
classroom use. Kokoç et al. (2025), in a meta-analysis of learning analytics interventions, found a 
small-to-moderate positive effect on academic performance, supporting its role in evidence-based 
instructional decisions and early identification of learners needing support. Kaliisa et al. (2024) 
offered a more cautious view, finding across 38 studies that learning analytics dashboards had not 

19
consistently lived up to claims of improving academic achievement, though they did show a 
relatively substantial, medium-to-large effect on student participation and engagement. Taken 
together, these reviews suggest that learning analytics is best positioned as a tool for sustaining 
engagement and informing teacher decisions rather than as a stand-alone driver of achievement 
gains, still a meaningful advantage over the manual tracking Phil-IRI currently requires.
Several AI-supported reading platforms currently demonstrate the practical application of 
these technologies, and independent evaluations offer evidence beyond the platforms’ own 
marketing claims. Amira Learning uses advanced speech recognition and real-time assessment to 
help teachers identify reading needs and adjust instruction accordingly, while providing continuous 
progress data (Amira Learning, 2018); in an independent randomized study of the automated 
reading-tutor technology underlying the platform, Mostow et al. (2013) found that struggling 
readers who practiced with the tutor scored significantly higher on a standardized reading 
composite than peers assigned to independent silent reading. Lexia Core5 Reading pairs adaptive 
skill practice with teacher-facing performance dashboards to support literacy growth across 
foundational skill areas (Lexia Learning, 2013); a subsequent independent evaluation by the RAND 
Corporation similarly found that students using Core5 achieved greater reading gains than matched 
comparison students who did not use the program (Pane et al., 2023). Read Along by Google uses 
speech recognition to give learners real-time pronunciation feedback and gamified rewards as they 
read aloud independently (Google LLC, 2019), and an independent classroom study by Sharma 
(2025) found measurable reading-level gains among young Hindi-language learners after several 
months of teacher-facilitated use. Raz-Kids offers a large leveled-reading library with basic 
comprehension quizzes and progress tracking, though it relies primarily on self-paced reading 
rather than AI-driven adaptive sequencing (Learning A-Z, 2002); an independent quasiexperimental study by Al-Riyami & Al-Siyabi (2024) found a noticeable, though not statistically 
significant, improvement in reading comprehension among EFL learners using the program 
compared to a control group. Read with Ello combines speech-recognition-based oral reading 
practice with a gamified rewards system designed specifically for beginning readers (Ello 
Technology, Inc., 2023); independent, peer-reviewed evaluation of the platform remains limited, 
with reviewers such as Common Sense Media (2024) noting the current absence of published 
outcome research despite the product’s Science-of-Reading-based design.
Each of these platforms demonstrates that AI can meaningfully personalize reading support 
and improve monitoring. None, however, was built as a Philippine-curriculum-aligned intervention 
system for beginning readers; they function as broad, generalized literacy tools, and no single one 

20
combines integrated speech-recognition-based oral reading assessment, gamified mastery 
activities, and curriculum-aligned intervention tracking within one Phil-IRI-responsive system.
Reading intervention is increasingly supported through educational technologies that 
address challenges in assessment, personalization, learner engagement, and instructional decisionmaking. The Department of Education (2024a) emphasizes the importance of strengthening 
foundational literacy through the MATATAG Curriculum, while the Philippine Informal Reading 
Inventory (Phil-IRI) continues to serve as the standard framework for identifying learners requiring 
intervention (Department of Education, 2024b). At the global level, UNESCO (2025) likewise 
recognizes technology-enhanced learning as an important means of expanding access to quality 
education and improving learning outcomes. In line with these priorities, TaraBasa AI integrates 
adaptive reading instruction, speech-recognition-based oral reading assessment, gamified learning 
experiences, mastery-based vocabulary development, and continuous learner progress monitoring 
within a single curriculum-aligned platform. By combining these functions, the system supports 
current efforts to strengthen foundational literacy while reducing the instructional and assessment 
burden experienced by teachers in Philippine public elementary schools.
In conclusion, effective reading intervention requires more than traditional classroom 
instruction alone. The literature reviewed demonstrates that adaptive learning, gamification, 
speech-recognition technology, learning analytics, and structured literacy instruction each 
contribute to improving reading outcomes. By integrating these evidence-based approaches within 
a unified platform, TaraBasa AI has the potential to support personalized reading intervention, 
timely learner assessment, and data-driven instructional decision-making while remaining aligned 
with the MATATAG Curriculum and the Phil-IRI framework. With continued development and 
classroom validation, the system can help strengthen foundational literacy and support teachers in 
delivering more efficient and effective reading intervention in Philippine public elementary 
schools.
Related Studies 
Recent developments in educational technology have led to the emergence of several AIsupported reading systems designed to improve literacy instruction through speech recognition, 
adaptive learning, digital practice, and learner progress monitoring. These platforms are relevant to 
TaraBasa AI because they demonstrate the capabilities of current intelligent reading technologies 
while also revealing the limitations that remain in addressing the needs of Philippine primary 
classrooms.

21
Google Read Along (Google LLC, 2019) is an AI-assisted reading application that uses 
speech recognition to support oral reading practice. It provides immediate pronunciation feedback, 
interactive reading activities, and gamified rewards that encourage children to practice reading 
independently. Independent pilot evaluations have reported improvements in reading proficiency 
and vocabulary-related outcomes among participating learners, suggesting that speech-based 
practice can effectively support early literacy development. However, Google Read Along 
primarily focuses on pronunciation and independent practice rather than comprehensive reading 
intervention. It also provides limited teacher analytics and lacks alignment with Philippine literacy 
intervention frameworks such as Phil-IRI and the MATATAG Curriculum.
Lexia Core5 Reading (Lexia Learning, 2013) is an adaptive literacy platform that 
personalizes instruction according to learner performance. The system continuously adjusts lesson 
difficulty, provides teacher dashboards, and generates detailed reports that support progress 
monitoring and instructional planning. Independent evaluations of Lexia Core5 have reported 
positive reading gains in school-based implementations, indicating that structured adaptive 
instruction can contribute to improved literacy outcomes. Compared with Google Read Along, 
Lexia Core5 offers more comprehensive adaptive instruction and stronger teacher support. 
However, it places less emphasis on speech recognition-based oral reading assessment and 
functions as a general literacy platform rather than a localized intervention system aligned with the 
instructional requirements of Philippine public elementary schools.
Raz-Kids (Learning A–Z, 2002) is a cloud-based digital reading platform that provides 
leveled e-books, reading assignments, and comprehension quizzes. It supports independent reading 
while enabling teachers to assign activities, monitor learner performance, and generate progress 
reports through an integrated dashboard. Its primary strength lies in expanding access to leveled 
reading materials and supporting differentiated instruction. Nevertheless, the platform does not 
incorporate artificial intelligence, speech recognition, or adaptive intervention mechanisms capable 
of delivering individualized reading remediation based on learner performance.
Read with Ello (Ello Technology, Inc., 2023) is an AI-powered reading coach that listens 
as children read aloud and provides personalized feedback through speech recognition technology. 
It also adapts reading lessons according to learner performance and recommends books that match 
each learner's reading level. By combining oral reading practice with adaptive guidance, the 
platform creates a more interactive reading experience than conventional digital reading 
applications. Despite these capabilities, Read with Ello functions primarily as an AI reading 
assistant rather than a comprehensive intervention system and does not fully integrate teacher 

22
support, parent involvement, curriculum alignment, or learner monitoring features required in 
Philippine elementary schools.
Amira Learning (2018) is an AI-powered reading platform that combines automated oral 
reading assessment with personalized literacy instruction. The system analyzes learners' oral 
reading performance, identifies strengths and areas requiring intervention, and provides teachers 
with instructional recommendations supported by detailed learner analytics. In addition to adaptive 
instruction, Amira integrates benchmark assessment, universal screening, dyslexia screening, and 
continuous progress monitoring, making it one of the most comprehensive AI-supported reading 
systems currently available. However, despite its extensive capabilities, it remains a commercial 
platform designed for a broad educational market and is not specifically aligned with Phil-IRI 
assessment procedures, the MATATAG Curriculum, or the instructional realities of Philippine 
public elementary classrooms.
Collectively, the reviewed platforms demonstrate that artificial intelligence, adaptive 
learning, speech recognition, and digital progress monitoring can significantly strengthen reading 
instruction. Google Read Along and Read with Ello are particularly effective in supporting oral 
reading practice through speech recognition, while Lexia Core5 and Amira Learning emphasize 
adaptive instruction, teacher analytics, and personalized intervention. Raz-Kids complements these 
systems by providing extensive digital reading resources and learner progress tracking. However, 
none of the reviewed platforms fully integrates adaptive reading intervention, speech recognitionbased oral reading assessment, mastery-based vocabulary development, gamified learning, teacher 
and parent monitoring, and alignment with Philippine literacy frameworks within a single platform. 
These limitations indicate that although existing systems successfully implement individual 
intelligent technologies, they do not provide a comprehensive reading intervention solution 
specifically designed for the instructional needs of Philippine primary education. This identified 
gap provides the rationale for the development of TaraBasa AI, an AI-powered adaptive reading 
intervention system that integrates speech recognition-based oral reading assessment, adaptive 
learning, mastery-based vocabulary development, gamified learning experiences, and continuous 
teacher and parent progress monitoring to provide personalized literacy support while aligning with 
the instructional goals of Phil-IRI and the MATATAG Curriculum.
