39
Table 4 visualizes the Business Model Canvas for TaraBasa AI, which serves three main 
customer segments: learners, teachers, and parents. For learners, it offers Phil-IRI-aligned AI 
reading assessment, adaptive reading activities, AI speech recognition, and a Mastered Word Bank 
through a unified mobile application. Teachers benefit from AI-powered reading intervention 
planning, learner progress monitoring, and automated reports that support reading instruction and 
compliance with the DepEd "No Read, No Move" policy. Parents receive SMS notifications and 
intervention alerts, allowing them to monitor their child's reading progress without requiring a 
smartphone or internet connection. The application, available on Android with a web-based teacher 
dashboard, is supported by partnerships with teachers, parents, Google Cloud, and Semaphore SMS 
Gateway, and generates revenue through subscriptions and advertisements. Key resources include 
AI models, cloud infrastructure, speech recognition technology, and Phil-IRI-based reading 
content, with core activities involving data gathering, AI model development, adaptive reading 
content development, interface design, and system integration. These are sustained by a structured 
cost framework that enables the development and delivery of an AI-powered adaptive reading 
intervention platform for Philippine public elementary schools.
Program Workflow
This section presents the program workflow of the proposed system. It illustrates the 
structured sequence of processes and activities that define how the system operates from start to 
finish. The workflow serves as a visual representation of the interaction between users and the 
system, showing the logical flow of information and system functions. It helps in understanding 
the relationships among different processes, identifying dependencies, and ensuring the efficient 
execution of tasks. This serves as a guide in the design, development, implementation, and 
evaluation of the proposed system.

40
Figure 3.1: Program Workflow (Learners)

41
Figure 3.2: Program Workflow (Learners) cont’d

42
Figure 3.1 to 3.2 illustrates the workflow of the Learner actor, beginning the moment a 
Learner accesses their profile through an avatar selection or QR code scan. The system checks 
whether the Learner already has an established mastery level; if not, a diagnostic test is 
administered to determine their starting reading level before the first activity is loaded. Once an 
activity is displayed, the Learner reads the assigned text aloud, and the system records the audio 
for AI-based assessment. If the audio recording is invalid, the Learner is allowed limited retry 
attempts before being directed to seek assistance. Valid audio proceeds to AI evaluation, where the 
Learner's reading accuracy determines whether their mastery level increases, remains the same, or 
decreases with a corresponding "needs attention" flag. The Learner then receives immediate session 
feedback, earns points, tracks their streak count, and may unlock a badge upon reaching a 
milestone, after which the Teacher and Parent are notified of the completed session.

43
Figure 4.1: Program Workflow (Teacher)

44
Figure 4.2: Program Workflow (Teacher) cont’d

45
Figure 4.3: Program Workflow (Teacher) cont’d

46
Figure 4.1 to 4.3 presents the workflow of the Teacher actor, beginning with login 
authentication and proceeding to the Teacher Dashboard upon successful verification. From the 
dashboard, the Teacher may manage classes — creating or editing a class, releasing a Learner for 
grade-level promotion, or claiming a Learner previously released by another Teacher. The Teacher 
may also join an existing Learner into their class using a Learner Code generated by the Parent. In 
generating reading activities, the Teacher selects a curriculum topic, after which the AI produces a 
draft activity for the Teacher's review; the Teacher may approve, edit, or reject the draft before it 
is assigned to a Learner and optionally published to the Open Repository. The Teacher may further 
review dashboard analytics for an individual Learner or a performance-based group, and manage 
notifications relevant to their role.

47
Figure 5.1: Program Workflow (Parents)

48
Figure 5.2: Program Workflow (Parents) cont’ d

49
Figure 5.1 to 5.2 details the workflow of the Parent actor. A Parent without an existing 
account first creates a Learner profile by entering the child's details, estimating their current reading 
stage, and completing a short AI-driven placement check to refine that estimate; the system then 
generates a unique Learner Code and assigns a PIN set by the Parent. Once logged in, if more than 
one Learner is linked to the Parent's account, a learner selection step precedes the dashboard. From 
the Parent Dashboard, the Parent may view the Learner's progress, initiate a practice session outside 
of school hours, browse the Open Repository for additional teacher-shared activities, complete 
payment for paid content, and manage their own notifications.

50
Figure 6.1: Program Workflow (Administrator)

51
Figure 6.1 presents the workflow of the Admin actor, whose role is intentionally limited to 
account verification and status management. After logging in, the Admin reviews pending Teacher 
accounts, verifying the submitted employee identification before activating or rejecting the account. 
The Admin may also select any existing account to activate or deactivate its status as needed, after 
which the Admin logs out to end the session.
