Experiment 1. Grade 1–3 teachers experience difficulty identifying struggling readers, 
conducting individual reading assessments, and monitoring each learner's reading progress due to 
large class sizes and the time required for manual assessment. As a result, struggling readers may 
not receive timely intervention, making it difficult to improve their reading proficiency. The 
proposed solution is the development of "TaraBasa AI," an AI-powered adaptive reading 
intervention system that provides automated reading assessment, adaptive learning activities, 
learner progress monitoring, and a real-time teacher dashboard. The pilot program will involve 
Grade 1–3 teachers from Suba Elementary School in Liloan, Cebu, who will evaluate the system's 
usefulness in reading assessment and learner monitoring. Success will be measured through teacher 
feedback on the system's effectiveness, ease of use, and its ability to improve the efficiency of 
reading intervention. Based on the survey, 90.9% of teachers agreed that an AI-powered reading 
assessment and progress monitoring system would be beneficial. The findings will be used to refine 
the teacher dashboard, assessment features, and learner monitoring tools before full system 
implementation.
Experiment 2. Parents and guardians often experience difficulty monitoring and supporting 
their children's reading development at home due to limited time, work responsibilities, and the 
lack of regular updates regarding their child's reading progress. These challenges reduce their 
ability to provide timely reading support and identify areas where their children need improvement. 
TaraBasa AI addresses this problem by providing an automated parent monitoring feature that 
sends reading progress reports and updates, allowing parents to stay informed about their child's 
reading development. The pilot program will involve parents and guardians of Grade 1–3 learners 
from Suba Elementary School, who will evaluate the usefulness of the progress monitoring and 
reporting features. Success will be measured through parent feedback regarding the accessibility, 
usefulness, and effectiveness of the automated progress reports in supporting reading activities at 
home. Survey results showed that 84.7% of parents agreed that an automated reading progress 
tracking system would help them stay informed about their child's reading development. The 

56
findings will guide improvements to the parent monitoring and communication features of the 
system.
Experiment 3. Grade 1–3 learners who struggle with reading often experience difficulties 
in phonics, letter-sound recognition, reading fluency, and reading confidence, causing them to fall 
behind in classroom activities and become less engaged during reading instruction. TaraBasa AI 
addresses these challenges by providing AI-powered adaptive reading activities with speech 
recognition and gamified learning that personalize reading instruction according to each learner's 
reading ability. The pilot program will focus on evaluating the proposed learner-centered features 
using teacher observations and feedback gathered during the validation stage. Success will be 
measured by determining whether the proposed adaptive learning activities address the reading 
difficulties commonly observed among learners. Survey findings revealed that 36.4% of teachers 
identified letter-sound and phonics difficulties as the most common reading challenge, while 63.6% 
identified phonics as the most important foundational reading skill that learners must master first. 
These findings validate the need for TaraBasa AI's adaptive, phonics-based, and gamified reading 
intervention approach and will guide further refinement of the learning activities before prototype 
testing with Grade 1–3 learners.
