This section presents the Gantt chart, which illustrates the project's tasks and their 
corresponding timelines using horizontal bars. It helps the researchers plan, organize, monitor, and 
coordinate project activities effectively while providing a clear overview of the project's progress. 
The Gantt chart also supports timely completion of project requirements by tracking the schedule 
of each task. Since the study follows the Agile methodology, the chart is used to outline the planned 
timeline for each sprint and development activity, making it easier to monitor progress throughout 
the project.
Functional Decomposition Diagram 
This section presents the functional decomposition diagram, which visually breaks down 
the major functions of the proposed system into smaller and more manageable components. It 
provides a clear overview of the system's processes, illustrating the relationship between each 
function and improving the overall understanding of how the application operates.
Figure 7.1: Functional Decomposition Diagram
Figure 7.1 illustrates that the "TaraBasa AI" application is composed of six key modules: 
Account Management, AI Activity Generation, Learner Reading Assessment, Open Repository 
Management, Dashboard Analytics, and Notification Management. These modules work together 
to deliver an integrated reading intervention experience, enabling account registration and 
verification for all four actors, AI-assisted content generation guided by a curriculum reference, 
individualized reading assessment for Learners, a teacher-driven content marketplace accessible to 

61
Parents, role-specific progress monitoring, and shared notification delivery across Teacher and 
Parent.
Figure 7.2: Functional Decomposition Diagram cont’d
Figure 7.2 presents the Account Management and AI Activity Generation modules. Within 
Account Management, the Admin is responsible for logging in and managing user accounts —
viewing pending Teacher accounts, verifying employee identification, and activating, rejecting, or 
deactivating accounts. The Teacher registers, logs in, views and edits their profile, creates or edits 
a class, adds a Learner to their class, releases a Learner for promotion, claims a previously released 
Learner, and joins an existing Learner using a Learner Code. The Parent registers, logs in, and is 
responsible for the creation of the Learner's account — entering the child's details, estimating their 
reading stage, generating the Learner Code, setting the Learner's PIN, running the placement check, 
and selecting a Learner profile if multiple children are linked. The Learner's only actions in this 
module are selecting an avatar, entering their PIN, and accessing their profile — reflecting the 
system's design principle that Learner accounts require no independent registration. In the AI 
Activity Generation module, the Teacher selects a curriculum topic, generates a draft activity 

62
through the AI engine, reviews the draft, edits or declines it as needed, and assigns the approved 
activity to a Learner.
Figure 7.3: Functional Decomposition Diagram cont’d
Figure 7.3 displays the Learner Reading Assessment and Open Repository Management 
modules. In Learner Reading Assessment, the Learner starts the diagnostic test, reads the assigned 
text aloud, submits the reading audio for AI evaluation, views session feedback, earns points, tracks 
their streak count, and unlocks badges upon reaching milestones. In Open Repository Management, 
the Teacher sets the price type of a shared activity and publishes it to the repository, while the 
Parent browses the shared activities, completes payment for paid content, and unlocks the activity 
for their Learner's use.

63
Figure 7.4: Functional Decomposition Diagram cont’d
Figure 7.4 presents the Dashboard Analytics and Notification Management modules. In 
Dashboard Analytics, the Teacher selects a Learner or performance-based group, displays the 
mastery trend, and displays session history, while the Parent displays the Learner's current mastery 
level and session history. In Notification Management, both the Teacher and the Parent may view 
their respective notification list and mark notifications as read — reflecting that both actors receive 
role-relevant updates through a single shared module rather than separate, actor-specific 
notification systems.
Analysis Design Phase 
During the Analysis-Design phase of TaraBasa AI, the researchers focused on identifying 
the project requirements and designing the overall system architecture. This involved gathering and 
analyzing user needs, project objectives, and technical requirements through consultations with 
teachers, parents, and school administrators. The information gathered helped determine the 
essential features of the system, such as AI-powered reading assessment, adaptive reading 
activities, pronunciation feedback, learner progress monitoring, and parent monitoring. After 
collecting the requirements, the researchers conducted a system analysis to identify both the 
functional and non-functional requirements, evaluate the feasibility of the proposed system, and 
define its overall scope. This phase also included the development of the Use Case Diagram, 
Functional Decomposition Diagram, Program Workflow, User Interface Design, Database Schema, 
and System Architecture to support AI integration, user interactions, and the web and Android 
platforms.

64
Use Case Diagram/DFD 
This section presents the use case diagram, which offers a visual representation of the 
system’s functional requirements and user interactions. For the TARABASA AI System, the 
diagram outlines various scenarios where users engage with the platform to complete AI-assisted 
reading assessments, manage reading assignments and track learner analytics, and oversee 
administrative records and content.
Figure 8.1: Use Case Diagram -Account Management

65
Figure 8.1 illustrates how Teacher, Parent, Learner, and Admin interact with the Account 
Management module. Teachers can register, log in, view and edit their profile, create or edit a class, 
add a Learner to their class, release a Learner for promotion, claim a promoted Learner, and join a 
Learner using a Learner Code. Parents can register, log in, enter their child's details, estimate their 
reading stage, generate a Learner Code, set the Learner's PIN, run the placement check, and select 
a Learner profile. Learners interact only through selecting an avatar, entering a PIN, and accessing 
their profile. Admins log in, manage user accounts, view pending Teacher accounts, verify 
employee identification, and activate, reject, or deactivate accounts. Log in and Log out are shared 
use cases connected to Teacher, Parent, and Admin, reflecting the common authentication pattern 
across those three actors.
Figure 8.2: Use Case Diagram cont’d

66
Figure 8.2 presents the remaining five modules in a consolidated diagram. Teachers select 
curriculum topics, generate, review, edit, approve, and assign learning activities to learners. They 
can also set the pricing of shared content, publish it to the Open Repository, and monitor learners' 
mastery levels and session history. Learners complete diagnostic tests, submit reading audio, 
receive session feedback, earn points, maintain streaks, and unlock badges. Parents can initiate 
practice sessions for their learners, browse and purchase activities from the Open Repository, and 
monitor their learners' mastery levels and session history. The View Notification List and Mark 
Notification Read use cases are shared by both Teachers and Parents, reflecting the platform's 
unified Notification Management module.

67
User Interface Design
The following user experience figures presented here are the User Interface Design of 
“TaraBasa AI" , showing the initial design on how the application would look and its 
functionalities. The User Interface’s layout, color scheme and experience are made to tailor the 
best.
Figure 9: Splash Screen
The Splash Screen is displayed when the application is launched. It features the TaraBasa 
AI logo, the Basa owl mascot, the application's name, a short tagline, and an animated loading 
indicator. This screen serves as the application's branding interface while the system initializes 
before automatically navigating to the Welcome Screen.

68
Figure 10: Welcome Screen
The Splash Screen is displayed when the application is launched. It features the TaraBasa 
AI logo, the Basa owl mascot, the application's name, a short tagline, and an animated loading 
indicator. This screen serves as the application's branding interface while the system initializes 
before automatically navigating to the Welcome Screen.

69
Figure 11: Parent Registration Screen
The Parent Registration Screen allows new parents to create an account by entering their 
full name, email address, password, and password confirmation. The screen also includes a Sign 
Up button and a navigation link for existing users to return to the Login Screen. Its simple form 
layout provides an easy and secure account registration process.

70
Figure 12: Login Screen
The Login Screen enables registered users to access the TaraBasa AI platform by entering 
their email address and password. It also provides options for password recovery and account 
registration. A friendly illustration of the Basa owl is displayed to create an engaging and 
welcoming interface for users.

71
Figure 13: Create Child Profile Screen
The Create Child Profile Screen allows parents to register their child before using the 
application. It includes an avatar selector, input fields for the child's name, age, grade level, school 
name, and language spoken at home. A Done! button is provided to save the child's profile and 
complete the registration process.

72
Figure 14: Parent Dashboard Screen
The Parent Dashboard Screen serves as the main interface for parents after logging in. It 
displays greeting information, notifications, child profile cards, weekly reading highlights, reading 
streaks, progress indicators, and a shortcut for viewing each child's progress. A dedicated Add 
Child button allows parents to register additional children under their account.

73
Figure 15: Learner Home Screen
The Learner Home Screen is the primary dashboard displayed to learner after selecting 
their profile. It presents the child's avatar, reading level, daily reading goal, earned coins, reading 
streak, available lessons, and a Start Reading button. A bottom navigation bar provides quick access 
to Home, Lessons, Rewards, Progress, and Profile.

74
Figure 16: Assessment Introduction Screen
The Assessment Introduction Screen prepares learners before beginning a reading 
assessment. It displays the Basa owl mascot, simple step-by-step instructions, the estimated 
assessment duration, and a I'm Ready! Let's Go! button. The screen familiarizes students with the 
assessment process before starting.

75
Figure 17: Reading Assessment Screen
The Reading Assessment Screen presents the reading activity where learners read words, 
sentences, or passages aloud. It includes a progress indicator, countdown timer, reading content, 
supporting illustrations, and a microphone button used for voice recording. A speech bubble from 
the Basa owl provides encouragement while the child completes the activity.

76
Figure 18: Comprehension Quiz Screen
The Comprehension Quiz Screen displays questions related to the completed reading 
passage. Each question is presented with multiple-choice answer cards that provide immediate 
visual feedback after selection. A progress indicator and Next button help students navigate through 
the quiz until completion.

77
Figure 19: Assessment Result Screen
The Assessment Result Screen presents the learner's reading assessment outcome using 
celebratory animations and visual rewards. It displays the learner's reading level, reading 
performance summary, earned stars, and encouraging feedback from the Basa owl. A Start 
Learning Now! button allows students to continue with personalized learning activities.

78
Figure 20: Lesson Result Screen
The Lesson Result Screen celebrates the learner's completion of a lesson. It displays earned 
stars, reading accuracy, collected coins, newly unlocked badges, and congratulatory messages 
accompanied by colorful animations. Navigation buttons allow students to proceed to the next 
lesson or return to the Home Screen.

79
Figure 21: Rewards Screen
The Rewards Screen displays the learner's achievements throughout the learning process. 
It includes the learner's avatar, accumulated coins, stars, reading streak, unlocked badges, and a 
seven-day reading calendar. The screen also allows students to customize their avatar while 
motivating continuous reading through a visual reward system..

80
Figure 22: Teacher Dashboard Screen
The Teacher Dashboard Screen serves as the main interface for teachers after logging in. 
It displays class overview cards containing the total number of students, students requiring 
intervention, average reading level, and completed assessments for the current week. Quick access 
shortcuts to learner management, reports, notifications, and reading activities are also provided, 
allowing teachers to monitor classroom performance efficiently.

81
Figure 23: Class List Screen
The Class List Screen displays all learners assigned to the teacher in a searchable and 
filterable list. Each learner card includes the learner's name, reading level badge, assessment status, 
and intervention indicator. Search and filter options enable teachers to quickly locate learners based 
on class, reading level, or assessment status.

82
Figure 24: Learner Profile Screen
The Learner Profile Screen provides detailed information about an individual learner's 
reading performance. It displays the learner's assessment history, AI-generated reading analytics, 
progress charts, reading level, and teacher notes. The screen enables teachers to review the learner's 
reading development and document observations for future reference.

83
Figure 25: Assign Reading Activity Screen
The Assign Reading Activity Screen allows teachers to create and assign reading activities 
to selected learners or classes. It includes options to choose reading materials, select the appropriate 
reading level, assign multiple learners, and specify a due date. A submit button publishes the 
activity, making it immediately available on the learners' dashboards.

84
Figure 26: Intervention Flag Screen
The Intervention Flag Screen displays learners identified by the AI as requiring additional 
reading support. Each learner entry includes the learner's reading level, assessment summary, 
identified areas for improvement, and recommended intervention strategies. A Message Parent 
button enables teachers to communicate directly with parents regarding the learner's reading 
progress and recommended support.

85
Figure 27: Reports and Analytics Screen
The Reports and Analytics Screen provides a comprehensive overview of class 
performance through charts and statistical summaries. It displays average reading scores, reading 
level distribution, assessment completion rates, and the most common reading difficulties identified 
by the AI. The visual reports help teachers monitor classroom progress and identify instructional 
priorities.

86
Figure 28: Notifications Screen
The Notifications Screen displays important updates related to classroom activities and 
learner assessments. Teachers receive notifications for completed reading assessments, newly 
assigned students, intervention alerts, and system announcements. Selecting a notification redirects 
the teacher to the corresponding student record or activity for further review.

87
Figure 29: Teacher Profile and Settings Screen
The Teacher Profile and Settings Screen allows teachers to manage their personal account 
information and application preferences. It includes options to edit profile details, change the 
account password, configure notification settings, and securely log out of the application. The 
organized layout enables teachers to personalize their account while maintaining account security.

88
Figure 30:Admin Login Screen (Web)
The Admin Login Screen allows authorized administrators to securely access the TaraBasa 
AI administration portal by entering their registered email address and password. It includes a 
password visibility toggle, a system notice indicating that only authorized administrators can access 
the portal, and a password recovery reminder. The clean interface provides a secure entry point for 
managing the entire system.

89
Figure 31: Admin Dashboard Screen (Web)
The Admin Dashboard Screen serves as the main interface for system administrators. It 
displays statistical cards showing the total number of schools, teachers, learners, parent accounts, 
active users, content items, and students flagged for intervention. The dashboard also presents 
charts for daily active users and reading level distribution, along with recent registrations and 
system activity logs, providing administrators with a comprehensive overview of the platform.

90
Figure 32: AI Configuration Screen (Web)
The AI Configuration Screen allows administrators to configure the system's AI 
assessment settings. It includes adjustable reading level thresholds, mastery requirements, fluency 
targets, gamification rewards, badge conditions, and assessment settings. The screen also displays 
informative notes and warnings to ensure administrators understand the impact of configuration 
changes before saving them.

91
Figure 33: Reports and Analytics Screen (Web)
The Reports and Analytics Screen provides a comprehensive overview of system-wide 
reading performance through statistical summaries, charts, and performance reports. It displays key 
performance indicators, reading accuracy trends, school performance comparisons, intervention 
reports, and content completion statistics. Date filters and export options allow administrators to 
generate reports for different reporting periods.

92
Figure 34: All Users Screen (Web)
The All Users Screen allows administrators to view and manage all registered users within 
the system. It includes search and filtering tools for locating users by name, role, status, or school. 
The user table displays profile information, account status, registration date, last activity, and 
available management actions such as viewing profiles, editing information, resetting passwords, 
deactivating accounts, and exporting user data.

93
Figure 35: System Logs Screen (Web)
The System Logs Screen provides administrators with a complete audit trail of activities 
performed within the system. It displays log entries containing timestamps, event types, user 
information, performed actions, activity details, IP addresses, and execution status. Search filters 
and export options allow administrators to efficiently review system activities, monitor security 
events, and maintain accountability across the platform.

94
Figure 36: User Detail Screen (Web)
The Reading Assessment Screen presents the reading activity where students read words, 
sentences, or passages aloud. It includes a progress indicator, countdown timer, reading content, 
supporting illustrations, and a microphone button used for voice recording. A speech bubble from 
the Basa owl provides encouragement while the child completes the activity.

95
Figure 37: Application Admin Storyboard
Figure 37 outlines the administrator workflow within the TaraBasa AI web platform. The 
administrator signs in through the Login Screen and is directed to the Dashboard, which displays 
system-wide statistics including total schools, teachers, learners, parent accounts, active users, and 
AI-flagged learners. An area chart, donut chart, and recent activity log provide real-time platform 
visibility. From the Dashboard, the administrator navigates to the All Users module to search, filter, 
and manage all registered accounts by role, school, or status. Selecting a user opens the User Detail 
screen, where the administrator can view and edit profile information, reset passwords, deactivate 
accounts, and review activity logs. For teacher accounts, linked class and student data are also 
accessible. The AI Configuration module allows the administrator to set reading level advancement 
thresholds, mastery and fluency targets, gamification reward values, badge unlock conditions, and 
assessment task counts. Changes take effect system-wide upon saving. The Reports and Analytics 
module presents performance data through KPI summaries, multi-level accuracy trend charts, 
school breakdowns, and intervention reports. Reports can be exported as PDF or CSV. The 
Announcements module allows the administrator to compose, schedule, and publish notices to 
teachers, parents, or all users. The System Logs module records all platform activity including 
logins, account changes, content updates, and AI configuration edits supporting auditing, 
monitoring, and troubleshooting. Together, these modules centralize all administrative functions 
for the secure and efficient operation of TaraBasa AI.

96
Storyboard
The storyboard is a powerful tool that summarizes the flow of the user experience in order. 
It provides a visual representation of the logical flow of the system to identify potential problems 
and ensure your app or website functions as intended. 
Figure 38.1: Learner Storyboard

97
Figure 38.2: Learner Storyboard cont’d
Figure 38 illustrates the storyboard for “TaraBasa AI” learner user workflow. The user 
journey begins at the gatekeeper screen, where new learners are prompted to register while 
returning learners can sign in directly using their credentials. After authentication, first-time users 
land on a diagnostic setup page titled "Let's Check Your Reading!" which establishes their baseline 
proficiency level. From this gateway, learners initiate the reading assessment process, navigating 
through sequential evaluation tiers. The system first presents a word-level flashcard paired with a 
visual illustration, prompting the learner to speak into their device microphone. Once processed, 
the workflow advances to sentence-level reading evaluation and eventually a short paragraph 
passage combined with a multiple-choice reading comprehension question.

98
The AI engine processes these interactive inputs and returns a personalized summary 
showcasing earned stars, reading milestones, and accuracy scores. Upon completion of the 
diagnostic benchmark, learners are routed to the core dashboard hub which displays their current 
level, reading streaks, and real-time progress loops. From this central interface, learners can pivot 
to the "My Progress" analytics dashboard to review deep learning metrics and visual charts of their 
reading activity, or navigate to the "My Lessons" module matrix to select specific unlocked reading 
cards. The storyboard further includes access to a gamified "My Rewards" showcase detailing 
accumulated badges and daily consistency streaks, alongside a profile management utility for 
application configurations. Through this sequence, “TaraBasa AI” provides an integrated, highly 
visual experience that connects interactive speech-to-text literacy assessment with automated 
student monitoring, localized performance analytics, and structured gamification milestones within 
a single child-friendly mobile platform.
Figure 39.1: Teacher Storyboard

99
Figure 39.2: Teacher Storyboard cont’d
Figure 39 illustrates the storyboard for “TaraBasa AI” teacher user workflow. The Teacher
journey begins at the main gatekeeper screen, where educators can select the dedicated teacher 
portal link to register a new institutional account or securely log in. Upon authentication, teachers 
land on the core teacher dashboard hub, personalized with their profile header (e.g., "Ms. Reyes"). 
This main interface provides an immediate analytical overview of the classroom, prominently 
displaying a high-priority metrics panel highlighting data such as the number of learners requiring 
immediate intervention, average class accuracy, and overall active engagement. Below this 
summary, a "Class Overview" list populates real-time learner statuses alongside their respective 
tracking profiles. From this central hub, teachers can navigate to a secondary "Notifications" panel 

100
to review system-generated updates, including automated intervention alerts, overdue reading 
assignments, and student level-up milestones.
Branching from the main dashboard, teachers can slide open a comprehensive 
configuration and utility menu. This sidebar allows them to toggle automated email alerts, push 
notifications, or weekly reporting summaries, while providing quick-action routing to four primary 
administrative modules: learner management, reading assignments, intervention flagging, and 
aggregate reporting. Selecting the analytics option routes the educator to a dedicated "Reports & 
Analytics" dashboard, which visualizes classroom performance macro-data through detailed 
weekly class accuracy bar charts, reading level distribution matrices, and a learner health status 
overview. Alternatively, entering the student management system opens a searchable, filterable 
"Learners" roster that breaks down individual metrics and allows data exports. Tapping on a 
specific learner profile, such as Maya Santos, opens an in-depth individual diagnostic screen 
showing localized performance warnings, accuracy trends over time, and automated "AI Learning 
Insights" tailored to that specific child's reading roadblocks.
The workflow further expands into actionable classroom management through the 
"Intervention Flags" and activity dispatch systems. The "Intervention Flags" screen groups 
vulnerable learners together, detailing explicit behavioral or accuracy markers and giving teachers 
direct options to view comprehensive technical reports or adjust specialized support rules. To 
provide targeted remediation, teachers can navigate to the "Assign Reading Activity" module. This 
intuitive, step-by-step workflow allows educators to filter their class by current level, multi-select 
specific learners via checkboxes, and pair them with contextual reading materials (such as “Level 
1: C-A-T The Cat” or “The Big Bug”) to deploy customized homework modules instantly. Through 
this synchronized, data-driven sequence, the teacher portal of “TaraBasa AI” transforms raw 
phonetic reading data collected from student speech modules into automated, structured 
pedagogical insights, bridging micro-level performance tracking with macro-level classroom 
interventions.

101
Figure 40: Parent Storyboard
Figure 40 illustrates the storyboard for “TaraBasa AI” parent user workflow as detailed in 
parent.png. The user journey begins at the main gatekeeper screen, where parents can choose to 
register a new account or log in with their existing credentials. After successful authentication, 
parents who are setting up the application for the first time are directed to the "Set Up Your Child's 

102
Profile" configuration screen. On this page, parents can customize their child's digital presence by 
selecting a friendly avatar, entering the child's name, selecting their age and grade level (Grade 1 
to Grade 3), and optionally adding their school name. Once the profile is finalized, parents land on 
the core parent dashboard, which serves as a personalized home hub greeting them by name.
From this central dashboard, parents can view a "My Children" management section where 
they can monitor active profiles, see quick snapshots like daily streaks, or use the "Add Child" 
feature to support multiple learners. The dashboard also highlights immediate operational insights 
through a "This Week's Highlights" summary panel, tracking parameters such as the last completed 
reading lesson, badges earned during the week, and total reading duration. Navigating from the 
main hub, parents can dive deeper into data by switching to the "My Progress" dashboard, which 
visualizes granular learning analytics for their child—including current reading milestones, a 
weekly activity bar chart, day-by-day reading accuracy percentages, and a breakdown of completed 
lessons. Furthermore, the workflow provides an integrated communication channel via the 
"Teacher Messaging" portal, listing active classroom instructors with an option to compose direct 
messages to streamline home-school collaboration. Through this comprehensive sequence, the 
parent module of “TaraBasa AI” Bridges the gap between early childhood home learning and 
structured academic monitoring within a centralized, insights-driven dashboard.

103
Database Design
This section refers to the process of creating a detailed data structure for storing, managing, 
and organizing data in a database management system (DBMS). It involves designing the layout, 
relationships, and rules that govern how data is stored and accessed within the database.
Figure 41: Entity Relationship Diagram

104
Figure 41 presents the Entity Relationship Diagram (ERD) of the TaraBasa AI platform, 
illustrating the structural relationships among the system's primary entities designed to support AIassisted reading assessment, adaptive gamified learning, teacher monitoring, parent 
communication, and repository-based reading intervention for Grade 1 to Grade 3 learners in 
Philippine public elementary schools.
At the center of the system is the User entity, which stores the fundamental account 
information of all registered users, including their name, email address, password, contact number, 
and user type (Administrator, Teacher, or Parent). The Teacher entity extends the User entity 
through a one-to-one relationship and stores teacher-specific information such as the employee ID, 
assigned school, and section.
Teachers manage one or more Class records, each representing a specific section or 
classroom. Every class contains multiple Learner records through a one-to-many relationship, 
allowing teachers to organize and monitor learners according to their assigned classes. Each learner 
maintains individual information such as grade level, Phil-IRI reading level, accumulated points, 
reading status, and other learning-related attributes used by the adaptive intervention engine.
The Activity entity serves as the primary repository of teacher-created reading activities. 
Each activity contains information such as the title, reading content, Phil-IRI level, MATATAG 
competency, language, difficulty level, AI-generated questions, and publication status. Teachers 
create and own multiple activities, while published activities become available to learners assigned 
to the appropriate classes.
To ensure curriculum alignment, the Curriculum Guide entity stores competency 
information, grade level, subject, and learning standards that teachers use when preparing reading 
activities. Teachers may upload curriculum guides, which serve as references for creating 
instructional materials that follow the MATATAG Curriculum.
Learners interact with the platform by completing reading activities. Every reading attempt 
is recorded in the Reading Session entity, which stores assessment results such as speech 
transcription, pronunciation accuracy, reading fluency, words correct per minute (WCPM), 
comprehension score, AI feedback, intervention recommendations, session date, and the learner's 
reading performance. These records provide the basis for monitoring learner progress and 
generating adaptive recommendations.
To encourage learner engagement, the system incorporates a gamification component 
through the Badge entity, which defines the available achievement badges and their corresponding 
point requirements. Since learners may earn multiple badges and each badge may be awarded to 

105
many learners, the Learner Badge entity resolves this many-to-many relationship by recording 
which learner earned each badge, together with the date awarded.
The platform also includes an Open Repository Listing entity that stores publicly available 
reading materials contributed by teachers. Learners may access these materials by unlocking them 
through the Repository Unlock entity, which records repository access and completed unlocks. This 
feature expands learners' available reading resources while encouraging continuous reading 
practice beyond teacher-assigned activities.
To further personalize reading intervention, learners maintain a Personal Word Bank, 
which stores unfamiliar or newly learned vocabulary encountered during reading sessions. This 
enables learners to build their vocabulary progressively while supporting future reading activities 
and intervention recommendations.
The relationship between parents and learners is managed through the Parent Learner 
entity, which resolves the many-to-many relationship between the Parent and Learner entities. This 
design allows parents to monitor one or more learners while permitting learners to be linked to their 
registered parent accounts. Parent communication is supported through the Notification entity, 
which records SMS notifications and weekly progress updates generated by the system regarding 
learner performance, intervention alerts, and important reading milestones.
Finally, the Promotion Record entity stores historical learner progression data, 
documenting the learner's completed grade level, promotion status, and academic year. These 
records preserve each learner's reading history even after advancing to the next grade level, 
allowing teachers and administrators to monitor long-term reading development.
Overall, the revised ERD demonstrates the modular and interconnected architecture of the 
TaraBasa AI platform, where user management, classroom organization, curriculum-aligned 
reading activities, AI-powered reading assessment, repository access, gamification, learner 
vocabulary development, parent communication, and learner promotion records are fully 
integrated. This structure enables reading performance data to flow seamlessly from AI assessments 
into teacher monitoring tools, adaptive interventions, and parent notifications, supporting timely, 
data-driven reading intervention for Grade 1 to Grade 3 learners in Philippine public elementary 
schools.
