Network Design 
This section includes the planning and implementation of “TaraBasa AI”'s computer 
network infrastructure. It involves evaluating, analyzing, and sizing the network to ensure it meets 
the needs of the application and its users. A well-structured network diagram representing the 
overall network architecture serves as a roadmap for physically assembling the network across the 
school, home, and cloud environments the platform depends on

124
Network Model 
A network model is a conceptual framework that describes the architecture, components, 
and interactions within a computer network. It outlines how different devices and services 
communicate with each other to achieve seamless connectivity and efficient data transfer.
Figure 42: Tarabasa AI Network Model
The network model for “TaraBasa AI” consists of a cloud-hosted web application and 
MySQL database connected via an ISP router at the data center, enabling secure communication 
over the internet. The cloud server also communicates outward with two external cloud services: 
the Google Cloud Speech-to-Text API, which processes learners' oral reading audio, and an SMS 
Gateway API, which dispatches text notifications to parents. Users, including the Admin, Teachers, 
Learners, and Parents, access the platform through separate ISP, school WiFi, or cellular 
connections linked to the internet. This setup ensures efficient data flow and reliable access to 
TaraBasa AI's core services, allowing teachers to monitor reading progress, learners to complete 
AI-assessed reading activities, and parents to receive timely SMS updates regardless of their device 
or connectivity level.
Network Topology
This section presents a visual illustration of the network topology, which represents how 
devices and components are integrated into a computer network. The topology diagram shows the 

125
different links between network nodes and helps in understanding the data flow, addressing 
mechanisms, and protocols for efficient data transfer.
Figure 43: "Tarabasa AI" Network Topology
The network topology of the “TaraBasa AI” platform centers around a centralized cloud 
server hosting the web application and MySQL database, which is reached through an ISP router 
at the data center. From this central server, two external services are called over the internet: the 
Google Cloud Speech-to-Text API for processing reading audio, and an SMS Gateway API, which 
routes messages through the cellular telecom network directly to a parent's mobile phone, allowing 
SMS delivery to reach even parents with basic, non-smartphone devices. On the access side, the 
Admin connects through a standard office or home ISP connection, while Teachers and Learners 
connect through a shared School Local Area Network at the partner school, where a school WiFi 
router links the teacher's PC or laptop and the learners' Android tablets to the internet through the 
school's own ISP connection. Parents with smartphones may additionally connect through mobile 
data or WiFi to access the optional web-based Parent Dashboard, entirely independent of the 
guaranteed SMS channel. This dual-path design ensures that no parent is excluded from reading 

126
progress updates due to lack of internet access or a smartphone device, directly supporting the 
platform's digital equity goals.
Development/Construction/Build Phase
During the development phase, everything required to implement the project is organized 
following the Agile Methodology adopted in Chapter 1. The research team coordinates with Suba 
Elementary School to confirm classroom access, learner participation, and device availability for 
pilot testing. Development environments are set up using XAMPP for local Apache and MySQL 
server hosting, Visual Studio Code for web development, and Android Studio for the mobile 
application. API access is arranged early in this phase, including registering for the Google Cloud 
Speech-to-Text API and securing an account with a Philippine SMS gateway provider, since both 
require account setup and, in some cases, verification time before development can proceed. A 
working timeline is established for each Agile sprint, covering account management features, the 
AI reading assessment engine, gamification features, teacher and parent-facing dashboards, and 
administrative tools, with testing and revisions incorporated at the end of each sprint.
Technology Stack Diagram
The table shown below is the technology stack diagram, which presents the needed 
requirements and technology for the proposed system, categorized into five sections: mobile 
application, web development, server, AI model, and future development.

127
